# Global product strategy for an adaptive-learning platform

## Executive recommendation

The startup should build **one adaptive-learning technology platform with country-specific learning systems layered on top**.

The governing principle should be:

> **Share the intelligence, infrastructure, and safety standards globally; localize curriculum, language, pedagogy, content, assessment, and purchasing workflows.**

The company should not merely translate its US product. Nor should it create five independent platforms. It should maintain:

- A common learner-model and adaptation engine;
- A common content-authoring and curriculum-mapping system;
- Shared security, privacy, accessibility, analytics, and AI governance;
- A global identity and entitlement layer;
- Country-specific curriculum graphs, content libraries, instructional languages, assessment models, teacher workflows, and commercial channels.

The preferred operating model is **“one platform, multiple nationally governed learning editions.”** Each edition should be co-designed with local educators and reviewed by an in-country curriculum and cultural council.

The central product promise should also vary by buyer:

- **Governments:** measurable curriculum coverage, implementation reliability, data governance, equity, and national capacity-building;
- **Schools and teachers:** time savings, actionable formative assessment, curriculum fit, and teacher control;
- **Parents:** understandable progress, safe and bounded screen use, affordability, and support for school learning;
- **Students:** relevant, accessible, motivating instruction without punitive or opaque automated decisions.

---

## 1. Define the shared core and local product layers

### Global product invariants

These should remain consistent across countries:

- The underlying learner-model architecture;
- Mastery estimation and recommendation infrastructure;
- Content metadata and version-control systems;
- Teacher ability to review and override recommendations;
- Explainability of why a lesson or exercise was assigned;
- Accessibility and inclusive-design baseline;
- Child-safety, privacy, and security standards;
- No advertising targeted at children;
- No sale of student data;
- No adaptation based on protected or irrelevant personal characteristics;
- No high-stakes decision made solely by the algorithm;
- Clear separation of learning analytics from commercial personalization;
- Audit logs for curriculum, content, model, and recommendation changes;
- Common APIs for rostering, identity, learning-management systems, and reporting;
- A portable but jurisdiction-controlled learner record where legally permitted.

### Country-localized layers

Each national edition should be able to vary:

- Mandated curriculum and grade structure;
- Instructional language and terminology;
- Reading direction, typography, examples, and voice;
- Sequencing constraints imposed by the curriculum;
- Assessment style and preparation for national examinations;
- Cultural, religious, historical, and civic content;
- Teacher and parent dashboards;
- Screen-time defaults and offline activities;
- Accessibility accommodations appropriate to local languages;
- School calendars, timetables, and reporting periods;
- Procurement, licensing, invoicing, and support arrangements;
- Data residency and retention configurations;
- Local integrations, payment methods, and pricing.

This structure avoids two common failures: treating a translated US product as local, or fragmenting the technology into costly country-specific codebases.

---

## 2. Build a curriculum-localization architecture

The platform’s most important local asset should be a **versioned curriculum graph**, not a collection of translated exercises.

### Curriculum graph

Represent each country’s curriculum as a structured graph containing:

- National curriculum authority and version;
- Grade, subject, strand, topic, and learning objective;
- Prerequisite relationships;
- Required instructional sequence;
- Expected proficiency level;
- Local assessment relevance;
- Required language of instruction;
- Approved or prohibited content conditions;
- Recommended instructional time;
- Associated lessons, questions, projects, and offline activities;
- Evidence required to demonstrate mastery.

The global skills taxonomy can sit below this layer. For example, the mathematical concept of fractions may be shared, while its grade placement, terminology, examples, teaching sequence, and assessment format differ by country.

### Many-to-many curriculum mapping

Do not assume that a US standard has a direct equivalent elsewhere. Each local learning objective should be mapped independently to:

- Global underlying skills;
- Local prerequisites;
- Required national outcomes;
- Local assessment formats;
- Appropriate instructional content.

Confidence levels and unresolved mappings should be visible to curriculum specialists. Machine-generated mappings can accelerate work, but local experts should approve them before release.

### Curriculum versioning

Government curricula change. The platform should support:

- Simultaneous operation of multiple curriculum versions;
- Migration plans for schools moving between versions;
- Effective dates by grade and region;
- A record of which students received which curriculum;
- Rapid correction of mapping or content errors;
- Procurement contracts tied to a specified curriculum version.

A curriculum update should not require rebuilding the adaptive engine or maintaining a separate application.

---

## 3. Adapt the adaptive engine itself

Localization must go beyond content. An engine trained and calibrated on US users may sequence lessons poorly for students taught under another curriculum or in another language.

### Keep globally shared

The company can share:

- Mastery-model architecture;
- Recommendation-service infrastructure;
- Experimentation framework;
- Model-monitoring tools;
- Content-quality scoring;
- General safety and explainability controls.

### Calibrate locally

Each national edition should calibrate:

- Prerequisite assumptions;
- Lesson sequencing;
- Difficulty estimates;
- Expected response times;
- Reading-level models;
- Error classifications;
- Mastery thresholds;
- Intervention recommendations;
- Language-specific speech or writing assessment;
- The role of repetition, projects, and test practice.

Difficulty should not be inferred solely from performance data imported from other countries.

### Teacher authority

The product should present itself as a teaching aid, not an autonomous instructor. Teachers should be able to:

- Assign or suppress content;
- Change sequence and pacing;
- Review the evidence behind mastery estimates;
- Correct the system’s interpretation;
- Group students without exposing stigmatizing labels;
- Pause adaptation during whole-class instruction;
- Export records in usable formats;
- distinguish “not yet assessed” from “not proficient.”

The platform should never silently prevent a learner from encountering mandated content because its model predicts that the learner is not ready. It can recommend remediation, but curriculum coverage and teacher judgment should control.

### AI safeguards

If generative AI is used for tutoring, translation, feedback, or content creation:

- Limit it to approved knowledge sources;
- Use age-appropriate interaction rules;
- Clearly identify AI-generated responses;
- Prevent it from replacing required teacher or safeguarding escalation;
- Log and test outputs by language;
- Require human approval for curriculum content;
- Provide reporting mechanisms for students and teachers;
- Avoid inferring disability, intelligence, motivation, or future educational potential from weak signals;
- Prohibit automated high-stakes grading or placement without meaningful human review.

Accuracy and safety testing must be performed separately in Vietnamese, Arabic, French, English, and Kiswahili. English performance is not a valid proxy.

---

## 4. Design a structured localization process

Every country edition should pass through six gates.

### Gate 1: Regulatory and procurement mapping

Map:

- Curriculum authority and approval requirements;
- Language-of-instruction rules;
- Private-school and international-school exceptions;
- Child privacy and education-data obligations;
- Cybersecurity and data-location requirements;
- Accessibility requirements;
- AI and automated-decision rules;
- Public tender and local-partner requirements;
- Tax, invoicing, subscription, and consumer-protection rules.

Requirements can differ between public schools, private schools, tutors, and direct household sales. Local counsel should validate the final position rather than relying on generalized country assumptions.

### Gate 2: Curriculum mapping

Local curriculum experts map every relevant learning objective and identify:

- Coverage gaps;
- Different prerequisite structures;
- Content that must be rewritten rather than translated;
- National-assessment implications;
- Teacher-training requirements.

### Gate 3: Linguistic and cultural production

Use professional educators and subject specialists, not translation alone. Review:

- Terminology used in local textbooks;
- Grammar and reading level;
- Names, images, maps, currencies, units, and examples;
- Religious, civic, historical, and geopolitical content;
- Voice, humor, gestures, dress, and family representations;
- Text-to-speech and speech-recognition quality;
- Right-to-left interface behavior for Arabic.

### Gate 4: Pedagogical validation

Test whether the product fits actual classrooms:

- Class size;
- Teacher workload;
- Device availability;
- Connectivity;
- Homework conventions;
- Assessment culture;
- Student familiarity with digital tools;
- Use of shared devices;
- Support for learners with disabilities.

### Gate 5: Government and stakeholder review

Where appropriate, obtain review from:

- Curriculum authorities;
- Local universities or teacher-training institutions;
- School leaders and teachers;
- Parent groups;
- Child-development and accessibility specialists;
- Cultural or religious reviewers;
- Privacy and security experts.

### Gate 6: Controlled pilot

Pilot by language, grade, subject, geography, school type, and connectivity level. Measure learning and implementation outcomes—not simply usage.

---

## 5. Country product playbooks

Country strategies should be treated as starting hypotheses and validated through local research. National markets are not culturally uniform.

### Vietnam

#### Product posture

Create a **Vietnamese-first, national-curriculum-aligned edition**, with English treated as a subject or separately approved bilingual pathway rather than the default interface.

Priorities should include:

- Alignment with the current national curriculum and local grade terminology;
- Vietnamese educational terminology and voice support;
- Strong teacher-assignment and class-monitoring tools;
- Structured practice that complements classroom teaching;
- Appropriate examination practice without reducing the platform to test drilling;
- Content review for history, geography, culture, maps, and civic material;
- Lightweight Android and web delivery;
- Downloadable lessons for inconsistent connectivity.

Parents may value visible academic progress and examination relevance, but the company should test this by segment rather than assume all households share the same expectations.

#### Commercial route

Use a dual path:

- School, tutoring-center, and education-partner distribution for trust and curriculum fit;
- A supplementary household subscription with transparent curriculum coverage and strong parental reporting.

A local academic or publishing partner can strengthen content credibility, support, and regulatory navigation. The company should verify applicable content-approval, cybersecurity, data, and consumer requirements before launch.

---

### Saudi Arabia

#### Product posture

Develop an **Arabic-first, right-to-left national edition**, with English-language pathways offered where consistent with the school’s curriculum.

Priorities should include:

- Modern Standard Arabic for core instructional content;
- Full right-to-left design rather than a mirrored afterthought;
- Alignment with the Saudi curriculum and school calendar;
- Local review of religious, civic, historical, visual, and social material;
- Arabic-capable tutoring and speech tools;
- Teacher-led configuration and institutional reporting;
- Family dashboards in Arabic and English;
- Accessibility and device support across school and home environments.

Cultural review should be formalized, versioned, and conducted by qualified local specialists. It should not rely on simplistic assumptions about Gulf families or schools.

#### Commercial route

Institutional sales are likely to be important, including schools, education groups, and government-linked procurement. The offering should therefore include:

- Tender-ready security and architecture documentation;
- Arabic implementation and support;
- Local hosting or data controls where required;
- Service-level commitments;
- Integration with institutional identity and learning systems;
- Train-the-trainer programs;
- A local commercial or implementation partner where useful.

A direct-to-consumer edition can support homework and supplementary learning, but it should not launch as an unaligned foreign curriculum product.

---

### France

#### Product posture

Position the platform as a **teacher-controlled, French-language tool aligned with the national curriculum**, not as a replacement for teachers or a high-intensity gamified screen product.

Priorities should include:

- French-first content and interface;
- Alignment with official curriculum cycles and terminology;
- Secular, institutionally neutral content;
- Strong accessibility and data-minimization controls;
- Short, purposeful digital sessions;
- Printable or teacher-led follow-up activities;
- Formative assessment rather than constant ranking;
- Clear explanations of recommendations;
- Minimal behavioral manipulation, streak pressure, or excessive gamification;
- Interoperability with school systems and common educational standards.

Because acceptance of educational technology may depend heavily on trust, the company should publish evidence, privacy documentation, and the pedagogical rationale for adaptation.

#### Commercial route

Public-sector procurement may be lengthy and administratively demanding. The company should maintain:

- A public-sector security and GDPR package;
- Clear controller/processor responsibilities;
- Data-protection impact assessment support;
- Subprocessor and data-transfer transparency;
- Accessibility documentation;
- French support and training;
- Contract terms suitable for schools and public bodies.

Private schools, tutoring organizations, and parent purchases can provide earlier market learning, but the consumer version should not bypass the standards expected in schools. Public procurement strategy should be developed with French education and procurement specialists.

---

### Kenya

#### Product posture

Build a **mobile-first, low-bandwidth edition aligned with Kenya’s current competency-based curriculum arrangements**, with both English and Kiswahili support where appropriate to subject, grade, and policy.

Priorities should include:

- Competency-based tasks rather than only multiple-choice practice;
- Teacher observation and portfolio tools;
- Support for projects and offline activities;
- Downloadable lesson packs and delayed synchronization;
- Shared-device and shared-classroom modes;
- Low-cost Android compatibility;
- Printable resources and SMS or lightweight parent notifications;
- Minimal media file sizes;
- Resilience to power and connectivity interruptions;
- Simple teacher workflows suitable for large classes;
- Inclusive content representing Kenyan settings and communities.

The platform should not interpret low interaction frequency as low motivation or ability when connectivity and device access may be the cause.

#### Commercial route

Use a blended model:

- Partnerships with schools, school networks, nonprofits, telecommunications providers, and public bodies;
- Affordable household subscriptions or learning packs;
- Locally suitable payments, potentially including mobile-money channels;
- School-site or classroom licensing where individual per-seat licensing is impractical.

Procurement assumptions should be validated at national and other applicable administrative levels. Hardware, connectivity, training, and support may need to be included in the implementation package rather than treated as the buyer’s problem.

---

### United States

The existing US edition should itself be modular rather than treated as the universal default.

It should support:

- State-specific standards and assessment mappings;
- District-level integrations and procurement;
- English learner and bilingual pathways;
- School and household privacy requirements;
- Teacher-led and direct-to-consumer editions;
- Accessibility and student-data protections;
- Variation in attitudes toward testing, AI, homework, and screen time.

The US skill taxonomy can contribute to the global core, but it should not define the international curriculum model.

---

## 6. Address parental attitudes toward screen time

A single engagement-maximizing product is unsuitable for education. The objective is learning effectiveness, not minutes consumed.

### Screen-time architecture

Provide configurable modes:

1. **Short-session mode**
   - Focused lessons with a clear stopping point;
   - Suitable for younger learners or screen-sensitive households.

2. **Classroom station mode**
   - Timed rotations among digital, teacher-led, collaborative, and paper activities.

3. **Offline extension mode**
   - Printable exercises, experiments, reading, projects, and discussion prompts;
   - Completion can be recorded later.

4. **Low-connectivity mode**
   - Lessons downloaded in advance;
   - Progress synchronized when connectivity returns.

5. **Assessment mode**
   - Distraction-reduced interface;
   - Explicit time and attempt rules;
   - No manipulative prompts to continue.

### Parent controls

Parents should be able to:

- Set or view session limits;
- Schedule available hours;
- See learning goals rather than only time spent;
- Distinguish active learning from passive video use;
- Receive plain-language progress summaries;
- Suppress nonessential notifications;
- Understand why the system recommended a lesson;
- Request deletion or correction where applicable.

Avoid streaks, countdowns, loss aversion, or notifications that pressure children to return merely to improve engagement metrics.

---

## 7. Support different attitudes toward standardized testing

The platform should separate its mastery system into three layers:

- **Curriculum mastery:** whether required concepts and competencies have been demonstrated;
- **Formative diagnosis:** what should be taught or practiced next;
- **Assessment familiarization:** practice in the format of a local examination where appropriate.

This prevents test preparation from dominating learning.

### Product rules

- Do not claim to predict official examination outcomes without strong validation;
- Label practice results clearly as unofficial;
- Do not use one country’s psychometric norms in another;
- Allow teachers to reduce or disable timed practice;
- Support project, oral, written, and observational evidence where curricula require them;
- Do not rank young learners publicly;
- Avoid repeated testing when sufficient evidence already exists;
- Explain uncertainty in mastery estimates;
- Separate diagnostic errors from language, connectivity, or interface problems.

Countries or buyer segments emphasizing examinations can receive relevant practice modules, while schools prioritizing competency development can emphasize portfolios, projects, and formative feedback. Both should use the same core learner model.

---

## 8. Create distinct products for procurement channels

The content and learning standards should remain consistent, but the buying and implementation experience should differ.

### Government edition

A government or large public-sector package should include:

- National curriculum coverage documentation;
- Local language and cultural review;
- Tenant-level configuration;
- Data-location and retention controls;
- Security and privacy documentation;
- Accessibility conformance;
- National, regional, school, and classroom reporting;
- Open APIs and export rights;
- Implementation and teacher-training plans;
- Local support;
- Service levels and disaster recovery;
- Evidence and pilot methodology;
- Transparent total cost of ownership;
- Contractual protection against vendor lock-in;
- A process for curriculum changes;
- Optional national content-authoring capability.

The startup should be ready for long sales cycles, tender requirements, budget timing, pilots, and implementation dependencies.

### School and school-network edition

Offer:

- Per-school, per-class, active-user, or site licensing;
- Rostering and single sign-on;
- Teacher dashboards;
- Assignment and intervention tools;
- Professional development;
- School-level reports;
- Family communication;
- LMS and student-information-system integrations.

Pricing should not penalize schools where students share devices or have intermittent attendance.

### Direct-to-consumer edition

Offer:

- Simple curriculum-based onboarding;
- Local payment methods and currencies;
- Monthly and annual plans without confusing renewal terms;
- Family accounts and sibling pricing;
- Parent-friendly progress reports;
- Short-session and screen-time controls;
- Easy cancellation and data deletion;
- A free diagnostic or limited learning tier;
- No advertising or sale of child data.

The consumer edition should not imply that more payment produces a more favorable mastery assessment.

### Channel-conflict rules

- A child’s mastery result must not differ because a parent purchased a premium plan.
- Essential accessibility and child-safety features should never be premium.
- Paid households may receive more content or tutoring, but not altered grades.
- DTC marketing should not undermine local teachers or official curricula.
- Schools should be able to transfer student work without exposing family purchase history.
- Government contracts should not require exclusive control over independently funded family learning unless legally necessary.

---

## 9. Establish country-level content and product governance

### Global Learning and Localization Council

Include:

- Product and engineering;
- Learning science and psychometrics;
- Curriculum specialists;
- AI governance;
- Privacy and security;
- Child safety;
- Accessibility;
- Regional leadership;
- Public-sector procurement;
- Teacher success and support;
- Local country representatives.

The council should define global standards and approve high-risk product changes.

### National curriculum councils

Each country should have a standing advisory and approval group with:

- Local teachers;
- Curriculum experts;
- Language specialists;
- School leaders;
- Parent or child-development representatives;
- Accessibility specialists;
- Cultural or religious expertise where relevant;
- Legal and data-governance support.

These councils should approve:

- Curriculum mappings;
- High-sensitivity content;
- Assessment claims;
- Major AI-tutor behavior;
- Language and cultural conventions;
- Changes that affect mandated curriculum coverage.

They should have genuine stop authority, not serve merely as a marketing endorsement.

### Content risk tiers

| Tier | Examples | Review |
|---|---|---|
| Low | Arithmetic practice, neutral interface text | Standard editorial review |
| Moderate | Local stories, family examples, assessment preparation | Local educator and language review |
| High | Religion, history, civics, maps, health, sexuality, politics, AI-generated tutoring | Formal national council, legal, safety, and cultural review |

---

## 10. Build trust through evidence and transparency

The company should avoid making a global “AI improves learning” claim based on US engagement data.

### Evidence ladder

1. **Usability:** Can students and teachers use the product?
2. **Curriculum validity:** Does it accurately cover local objectives?
3. **Measurement validity:** Do mastery scores reflect the intended local skill?
4. **Implementation evidence:** Can schools deploy it under real conditions?
5. **Learning impact:** Does it improve outcomes compared with an appropriate alternative?
6. **Equity impact:** Does it work across language, gender, disability, geography, income, device, and connectivity groups?
7. **Long-term effects:** Does learning transfer beyond short-term platform performance?

Where possible, use local universities or independent evaluators. Publish limitations, attrition, implementation conditions, and subgroup results rather than only headline averages.

### User-facing transparency

Teachers and parents should be able to understand:

- What data is collected;
- Why it is collected;
- How mastery is estimated;
- Why a recommendation was made;
- Whether AI was involved;
- How long data is retained;
- Who can access it;
- How to correct or challenge a result;
- Whether data is used to train models.

---

## 11. Commercial and pricing strategy

Use a local value architecture rather than converting US prices at current exchange rates.

### Pricing variables

Consider:

- Household purchasing power;
- School funding model;
- Tax and payment costs;
- Device sharing;
- Connectivity costs;
- Teacher-training and implementation needs;
- Support obligations;
- Procurement and payment cycles;
- Local partner margins;
- Currency volatility.

### Recommended product packaging

- **Household Basic:** limited subjects, parent reporting, offline access;
- **Household Plus:** broader curriculum and optional tutoring;
- **Teacher:** classroom assignments and formative reporting;
- **School:** administration, rostering, integrations, and training;
- **Government/Network:** enterprise controls, reporting, local authoring, implementation, and service commitments.

Avoid personalized prices based on a family’s inferred willingness to pay. Use transparent regional price books, scholarships, institutional subsidies, or verified eligibility programs.

---

## 12. Metrics and warning indicators

### Learning quality

- Mastery growth by curriculum objective;
- Retention and transfer of learning;
- Teacher-confirmed accuracy of recommendations;
- Curriculum coverage;
- Diagnostic false-positive and false-negative rates;
- Performance by language and learner subgroup;
- Difference between platform and independent assessment results.

### Adoption and implementation

- Teacher activation and continued use;
- Time required for classroom setup;
- Assignment completion;
- Offline synchronization success;
- Support requests;
- Training completion;
- School renewal;
- Parent understanding and satisfaction.

### Child and family experience

- Average session length;
- Frequency of sessions exceeding recommended limits;
- Parent changes to screen settings;
- Student frustration and repeated-failure patterns;
- Notification opt-outs;
- Complaints about pressure or excessive use;
- Unauthorized purchases;
- Safeguarding reports.

### Commercial performance

- Revenue and retention by channel;
- Government sales-cycle length;
- Pilot-to-contract conversion;
- Customer-acquisition cost by DTC market;
- Implementation cost per school;
- Gross margin after localization and support;
- Payment failure and currency risk;
- Revenue concentration by customer.

### Governance red flags

Trigger review when:

- Local teachers find material curriculum gaps;
- One language version performs materially worse;
- The model repeatedly assigns content outside the mandated sequence;
- Screen time rises without learning improvement;
- Mastery scores differ significantly by device or connectivity quality;
- AI generates culturally inappropriate or unsafe content;
- A government buyer requests surveillance or inappropriate student profiling;
- Procurement customization threatens to create a separate codebase;
- DTC marketing conflicts with teacher or curriculum messaging;
- A model update changes results without validated local testing.

---

## 13. Implementation roadmap

### First 90 days: build the operating foundation

- Select initial grades and subjects rather than localizing the entire US catalog;
- Document global product invariants;
- Establish the global and national governance councils;
- Map legal, curriculum, procurement, privacy, and language requirements;
- Create the curriculum-graph and content-versioning architecture;
- Audit existing models for US-specific assumptions;
- Define country-specific pilot hypotheses;
- Recruit local curriculum, language, and implementation partners.

### Months 3–6: create minimum viable national editions

- Map priority curriculum units;
- Build Vietnamese, Arabic, French, English, and Kiswahili localization pipelines;
- Implement right-to-left and low-bandwidth support;
- Create teacher override and recommendation-explanation tools;
- Add offline and printable learning modes;
- Produce public-sector privacy, security, and accessibility packages;
- Configure local parent dashboards and screen-time controls;
- Test language-specific model performance.

### Months 6–12: pilot and validate

- Run limited pilots across different school and household conditions;
- Compare platform mastery with teacher and independent evidence;
- Test DTC positioning separately from school positioning;
- Measure connectivity, device, and teacher-workload effects;
- Complete local content and cultural review;
- Refine pricing and implementation models;
- Obtain required approvals and procurement registrations;
- Publish pilot findings, including limitations.

### Months 12–24: scale selectively

- Expand only the countries, grades, and subjects showing curriculum validity and sustainable implementation;
- Build local teacher communities and train-the-trainer programs;
- Add institutional integrations;
- Establish local support capacity;
- Commission independent outcome studies;
- Enable approved local content authoring;
- Continue recalibrating models with governed local data;
- Retire country customizations that cannot be supported through the modular platform.

---

## Key decisions

| Decision | Recommended posture |
|---|---|
| Global platform | One technology, safety, identity, authoring, and adaptive-learning core |
| Curriculum | Separate, versioned national curriculum graphs |
| Localization | Rewrite and validate instruction; do not merely translate |
| Adaptive models | Share architecture but calibrate and test by country and language |
| Teacher role | Teacher-controlled recommendations with review and override |
| Government procurement | Enterprise edition with implementation, reporting, security, and local support |
| Direct-to-consumer | Parent-centered supplementary product with local pricing and screen controls |
| Screen time | Optimize for learning, with bounded sessions and offline extensions |
| Testing | Separate formative mastery from local exam familiarization |
| AI | Approved sources, local-language testing, human review, and no autonomous high-stakes decisions |
| Data | Global high-protection baseline plus jurisdiction-specific controls |
| Cultural fit | In-country expert governance and user research, not stereotypes |
| Connectivity | Offline-first and shared-device support where needed |
| Evidence | Validate curriculum, measurement, learning, and equity locally |
| Scaling | Expand by validated grade and subject, not by translating the full catalog at once |

## Conclusion

The startup should not export an American educational product with translated interfaces. Its scalable asset should be a shared adaptive-learning platform capable of hosting **genuinely national learning editions**.

Curriculum graphs, language models, instructional content, assessment formats, screen-time patterns, teacher workflows, and procurement packages should be localized. The learner-model architecture, content infrastructure, safety controls, accessibility baseline, explainability, and governance mechanisms should remain shared.

This model gives Vietnam a Vietnamese curriculum-aligned experience, Saudi Arabia an Arabic-first and locally reviewed edition, France a teacher-controlled and privacy-forward platform, and Kenya a competency-based, bilingual, low-bandwidth product—without creating four unrelated technology stacks. The result is local educational legitimacy combined with global technical leverage.

